# A Photonic Quantum Information Interface

S. Tanzilli\({}^{1,*}\), W. Tittel\({}^{1}\), M. Halder\({}^{1}\), O. Alibart\({}^{1}\), P. Baldi\({}^{2}\), Nicolas Gisin\({}^{1}\), Hugo Zbinden\({}^{1}\)

\({}^{1}\) Group of Applied Physics, University of Geneva, 20 rue de l'École de Médecine, CH-1211 Geneva 4, Switzerland
\({}^{2}\) Laboratoire de Physique de la Matière Condensée, Université de Nice-Sophia Antipolis, Parc Valrose, 06108 Nice Cedex 2, France

*sebastien.tanzilli@physics.unige.ch

######## Abstract

We report on a qubit transfer between photons at 1310 and 710 nm via a nonlinear up-conversion process with a success probability greater than 5%. In the event of a successful qubit transfer, we observe strong two-photon interference between the 710 nm photon and a third photon at 1550 nm, initially entangled with the 1310 nm photon, although they never directly interacted. The corresponding fidelity is higher than 98%.

## 1 Theoretical framework

Consider three systems labelled \(A\), \(B\), \(B'\). Initially \(B'\) is in the vacuum state \(|0\rangle\) while \(A\) and \(B\) are entangled with Schmidt coefficients \(c_1, c_2\):

\[|\Psi\rangle_{\text{in}} \;=\; \bigl(c_1|\alpha_1\rangle_A\otimes|\beta_1\rangle_B \;+\; c_2|\alpha_2\rangle_A\otimes|\beta_2\rangle_B\bigr)\otimes|0\rangle_{B'} \tag{1}\]

Target transferred state:

\[|\Psi\rangle_{\text{transfer}} \;=\; |0\rangle_B \otimes\bigl(c_1|\alpha_1\rangle_A\otimes|\beta_1\rangle_{B'} \;+\; c_2|\alpha_2\rangle_A\otimes|\beta_2\rangle_{B'}\bigr) \tag{2}\]

Effective Hamiltonian:

\[H \;=\; \mathbb{1}_A\otimes\Bigl(g_1|0\rangle_B\langle\beta_1|\otimes|\beta_1\rangle_{B'}\langle 0| \;+\; g_2|0\rangle_B\langle\beta_2|\otimes|\beta_2\rangle_{B'}\langle 0|\Bigr) \;+\; \text{h.c.} \tag{3}\]

Evolution:

\[e^{-iHt}|\Psi\rangle_{\text{in}} = \cos(|g_1|)c_1|\alpha_1\rangle_A\otimes|\beta_1\rangle_B\otimes|0\rangle_{B'} - i\frac{\sin(|g_1|)}{|g_1|}g_1 c_1|\alpha_1\rangle_A\otimes|0\rangle_B\otimes|\beta_1\rangle_{B'} \tag{4}\]

\[\hphantom{e^{-iHt}|\Psi\rangle_{\text{in}} =}\; + \cos(|g_2|)c_2|\alpha_2\rangle_A\otimes|\beta_2\rangle_B\otimes|0\rangle_{B'} - i\frac{\sin(|g_2|)}{|g_2|}g_2 c_2|\alpha_2\rangle_A\otimes|0\rangle_B\otimes|\beta_2\rangle_{B'} \tag{5}\]

Perfect fidelity requires \(g_1 = g_2 \equiv g\); at \(|g|t = \pi/2\) the transfer probability is unity.

## 2 Source and measurement

CW pump laser diode at \(\lambda_p = 711.6\) nm, \(L_c^p > 300\) m, feeds PPLN/W1, generating a photon pair at \(1312\) and \(1555\) nm. Franson analyzers on Alice's and Bob's sides, path-length difference \(\Delta L_A \simeq \Delta L_B \equiv \Delta L \sim 20\) cm.

## 3 Up-conversion stage

Second PPLN/W2 waveguide plus power reservoir (PR) CW laser at \(\lambda_{PR}=1560\) nm from an EDFA, delivering 700 mW. Internal up-conversion efficiency 80 %/W. Product photon at \(\lambda_{B'} = 712.4\) nm.

## 4 Success-probability estimate

\[P_{\text{success}} \;\approx\; 80\%/\text{W}\cdot 0.7\text{ W}\cdot(0.4)^2\cdot\frac{712\text{ nm}}{1312\text{ nm}} \;\approx\; 5\%\]

## 5 Interference visibilities

- Source (before QI): net \(V = 97.0 \pm 1.1\%\), raw \(V = 87.4 \pm 1.1\%\).
- After up-conversion: net \(V = 96.2 \pm 0.4\%\), raw \(V = 86.4 \pm 0.4\%\).

Deduced fidelity: \(F = (1+V_{\text{net}})/2 = 98.5\%\) (headline > 98 %).

